in Throw, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "17 – 20 August 2016"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/909"} "Estádio Olímpico João Havelange, Engenho de Dentro, Rio de Janeiro"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/358840"} "37 from 23 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Early in 2016 German " [:a {:href "/athletes/134129"} "Thomas Röhler"] " was considered the favorite as he had won two Diamond League meets and had four of the top seven throws in the world, but as Rio approached, back problems made him an unsteady favorite. He made the final in Rio, and was joined there by two other major challengers – defending gold medallist " [:a {:href "/athletes/125971"} "Keshorn Walcott"] " of Trinidad &amp; Tobago and 2015 World Champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/127005"} "Julius Yego"] " of Kenya."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Yego took the lead in round one with 88.24, which would remain his best mark and would eventually bring him the silver medal. It was also his only fair throw, as he passed on three other throws, including his final two, the victim of a chronic ankle injury. He would be taken off the field in a wheelchair."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In round two Walcott threw 85.38, his best effort, which was good for bronze. Yego held the lead, despite getting only one mark, into round five, when Röhler, until then in second place with 87.40 from the first round, came through with 90.30 to win gold. Despite Röhler’s consistency in invitationals, this was his first medal in a major international meet."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2515"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/134129"} "Thomas Röhler